---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

If ticks <= 0, return. Otherwise disable interrupts, set wakeup_ticks = timer_ticks () + ticks, list_insert_ordered into sleep_list, thread_block (), restore level. Handler: ticks++, then pop and unblock every sleeper whose deadline <= ticks, then thread_tick ().

>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?

The sorted list plus early exit means the handler only touches threads that actually expire, and costs one comparison when none do. No allocation, locks, or schedule (); preemption is deferred with intr_yield_on_return (), and deadline arithmetic stays in thread context.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?

sleep_list is the only shared datum and every access runs with interrupts off — mutual exclusion on a single CPU. A lock is impossible here: thread_block requires interrupts off, and the wakeup path executes in interrupt context, where blocking is illegal.

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?

The window between computing the deadline and blocking is the hazard: an interrupt there would unblock a still-RUNNING thread and trip thread_unblock's assertion. Disabling interrupts across compute+insert+block makes the two events mutually exclusive. The handler must not re-disable.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

Chosen over the busy-wait (spins the CPU, starves idle) and over a per-tick scan of all threads (O(threads) every tick). The ordered list gives O(1) per waking thread and a single comparison when nobody wakes. A separate sleep_elem avoids corrupting ready_list/waiters.

