/* threads/thread.h */
int priority;               /* EFFECTIVE priority: what the scheduler sorts on */
int base_priority;          /* requested by the thread; written only by
                               thread_set_priority */
struct lock *waiting_lock;  /* lock we are blocked on; edge for upward cascade */
struct list donations;      /* locks donating to us; scanned for the maximum */

struct lock;                /* forward decl; avoids prototype-scope warnings */
bool priority_more (...);            /* descending priority */
void thread_update_priority (struct thread *t);          /* recompute effective */
void thread_donate_priority (struct thread *h, struct lock *l); /* + cascade */

/* threads/synch.h */
struct lock {
  struct thread *holder;    /* NULL when free */
  struct semaphore semaphore;
  int max_priority;         /* highest priority among this lock's waiters */
  bool in_donations;        /* is elem linked into holder->donations? */
  struct list_elem elem;    /* node in holder->donations (its own elem) */
};

/* threads/synch.c */
struct semaphore_elem { struct list_elem elem; struct thread *thread; int priority; };

```

>> B2: Explain the data structure used to track priority donation.
>> Use ASCII art to diagram a nested donation.  (Alternately, submit a
>> .png file.)

```
   H  base 30  eff 30    BLOCKED on B        (donates 30 to M)
   | waiting_lock = &B
   v
 lock B  holder=M  max_priority=30  elem --> M.donations
   |
   v
   M  base 20  eff 30    BLOCKED on A        (donates 30 to L)
   | waiting_lock = &A
   v
 lock A  holder=L  max_priority=30  elem --> L.donations
   |
   v
   L  base 10  eff 30    RUNNING             (inherits the whole chain)

```

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B3: How do you ensure that the highest priority thread waiting for
>> a lock, semaphore, or condition variable wakes up first?

Waiter lists are kept priority-ordered on insertion and re-sorted immediately before waking, because donation can raise a waiter's priority after it queued — exactly what broke priority-donate-sema. Same for semaphores, locks (binary semaphores), and condvar waiters ordered by sema_elem.

>> B4: Describe the sequence of events when a call to lock_acquire()
>> causes a priority donation.  How is nested donation handled?

If the lock has a holder: record waiting_lock, raise lock->max_priority, link the lock into holder->donations, recompute the holder's effective priority. Nested case: if it rose and the holder is itself waiting on a lock, propagate to that lock's max_priority and its holder, recursively — only while rising.

>> B5: Describe the sequence of events when lock_release() is called
>> on a lock that a higher-priority thread is waiting for.

Unlink the lock from the donations list, clear max_priority, clear holder (before waking), recompute own priority from the remaining donations, then sema_up wakes the highest waiter; yield (or intr_yield_on_return) if it outranks us.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B6: Describe a potential race in thread_set_priority() and explain
>> how your implementation avoids it.  Can you use a lock to avoid
>> this race?

Priority has two writers — thread_set_priority and donation — so write-base/recompute/yield is not atomic: a donation can be lost or we yield needlessly. We disable interrupts across the whole sequence and only ever write base_priority. A lock cannot work: donations arrive in interrupt context, and blocking recreates the inversion.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B7: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

Base/effective split stops donation being overwritten; max-not-sum stops inflation; per-lock bookkeeping makes withdrawal exact; unconditional sema_down preserves mutual exclusion (making it conditional broke 6-of-7 silently); fixed release order avoids stale holders; > (not !=) makes the cascade terminate.

