>> C3: Did any ambiguities in the scheduler specification make values
>> in the table uncertain?  If so, what rule did you use to resolve
>> them?  Does this match the behavior of your scheduler?

Ambiguous: tie-breaking, whether tick 0 recomputes, and increment-versus-recompute order. Our rules: only a strictly higher ready thread preempts (the running thread keeps the CPU on ties); stable sort preserves FIFO, so B precedes C; the tick's +1 is applied before the 4-tick recompute. This matches our scheduler.

>> C4: How is the way you divided the cost of scheduling between code
>> inside and outside interrupt context likely to affect performance?

Cheap and frequent inside the handler beats expensive and batched: one fixed-point add per tick, O(n) recompute plus one ready-list sort per 4 ticks, O(n) decay per second. Deferring them outside interrupt context would leave the running thread's priority stale for several ticks.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> C5: Briefly critique your design, pointing out advantages and
>> disadvantages in your design choices.  If you were to have extra
>> time to work on this part of the project, how might you choose to
>> refine or improve your design?

Advantages: single writer for priority, small diff from part B, integer-only arithmetic. Disadvantages: O(n) scan and full sort in interrupt context every 4 ticks, only one ready list instead of per-priority queues, donation disabled so inversion is possible. With more time: per-level queues and dirty-flag recomputation.

>> C6: The assignment explains arithmetic for fixed-point math in
>> detail, but it leaves it open to you to implement it.  Why did you
>> decide to implement it the way you did?  If you created an
>> abstraction layer for fixed-point math, that is, an abstract data
>> type and/or a set of functions or macros to manipulate fixed-point
>> numbers, why did you do so?  If not, why not?

A header-only ADT of static inline functions names the representation once, forces the int64_t intermediate, keeps trunc and round as distinct named operations, and avoids multiple-definition link errors at zero runtime cost. Inline (a*b)/f at each call site invites unit and overflow mistakes. Floating point is unavailable in the kernel.

